{
  "title": "DIC: clotting, consumption and bleeding",
  "sources": [
    "CH29S117",
    "CH29S118",
    "CH29S119",
    "CH29S120"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Local repair reference",
      "text": "Normal clotting confines fibrin formation to a vessel injury. This scholarly repair figure provides the reference. Disseminated intravascular coagulation changes the location and extent of that process."
    },
    {
      "start": 12.64,
      "title": "Widespread activation",
      "text": "In DIC, widespread tissue factor exposure activates clotting. Thrombin and fibrin activity increase. Fibrin deposits inside small vessels impede blood flow, reducing organ perfusion."
    },
    {
      "start": 24.28,
      "title": "Consumption and bleeding",
      "text": "As clotting continues, platelets and coagulation factors are consumed. Accelerated fibrinolysis accompanies the process. The same patient can therefore have microvascular thrombosis and uncontrolled bleeding."
    },
    {
      "start": 37.08,
      "title": "Treat the driver",
      "text": "Treatment goals include eliminating the underlying pathology, restoring hemostasis and supporting failing organs. Component replacement alone does not remove the trigger. Chapter twenty nine, slides one hundred seventeen through one hundred twenty, support this sequence. The complete diagnostic laboratory pattern and specific treatment indications remain textbook gaps."
    }
  ],
